  <middle>
    <section anchor="introduction" numbered="true" removeInRFC="false" toc="include" pn="section-1">
      <name slugifiedName="name-introduction">Introduction</name>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-1-1"><xref target="RFC5785" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC5785"/> defines a path prefix, &quot;/.well-known&quot;, that can be used by
well-known URIs.  It also contains an IANA registry for URI suffixes
to be used with this path prefix for forming well-known URIs.</t>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-1-2">In <xref target="RFC5785" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC5785"/>, this mechanism is specifically defined for the &quot;http&quot;
and &quot;https&quot; URI schemes (now defined in <xref target="RFC7230" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC7230"/>).  Other URI
schemes such as &quot;coap&quot; and &quot;coaps&quot; <xref target="RFC7252" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC7252"/> have since picked up
this mechanism, sharing the registry of URI suffixes with that for
HTTP(S).</t>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-1-3"><xref target="RFC6455" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC6455"/>, which defines the URI schemes defined for the WebSocket
Protocol, &quot;ws&quot; and &quot;wss&quot;, does not define the use of well-known URIs
for these URI schemes.</t>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-1-4">The present memo formally updates <xref target="RFC6455" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC6455"/> to extend the use of
<xref target="RFC5785" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC5785"/> well-known URIs to the URI schemes &quot;ws&quot; and &quot;wss&quot;.</t>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-1-5">Well-known URIs for &quot;ws&quot; and &quot;wss&quot; share the registry for URI
suffixes established by <xref target="RFC5785" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC5785"/>; no change is necessitated by the
present memo in the registry or its IANA considerations.</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="iana-considerations" numbered="true" removeInRFC="false" toc="include" pn="section-2">
      <name slugifiedName="name-iana-considerations">IANA Considerations</name>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-2-1">This document does not require any IANA actions.</t>
    </section>
    <section anchor="security-considerations" numbered="true" removeInRFC="false" toc="include" pn="section-3">
      <name slugifiedName="name-security-considerations">Security Considerations</name>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-3-1">The Security Considerations of <xref target="RFC5785" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedContent="RFC5785"/> apply and need to be
considered for all well-known URIs.</t>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-3-2">It has always been possible to form &quot;ws&quot; and &quot;wss&quot; URIs in such a way
that they map to well-known HTTP(S) URIs when using the procedure in
<xref target="RFC6455" section="4" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedLink="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-4" derivedContent="RFC6455"/>; no new security considerations are created by
now formally making the well-known URI mechanism available for &quot;ws&quot;
and &quot;wss&quot;.</t>
      <t indent="0" pn="section-3-3">However, with well-known URIs becoming available for the WebSocket
Protocol, applications that want to define well-known URI suffixes
specifically for WebSocket use also need to consider whether the
resources becoming available under the equivalent HTTP(S) URI formed
by <xref target="RFC6455" section="4" format="default" sectionFormat="of" derivedLink="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455#section-4" derivedContent="RFC6455"/> pose any information disclosure or other
security considerations.</t>
    </section>
